# Architecture Compliance

This standard defines the Enterprise Security Monitoring & SIEM Architecture for the MFM Enterprise Platform.

General Enterprise Architecture principles are inherited from EA-020.

Enterprise Architecture positioning is inherited from EA-111.

Enterprise Event Architecture principles are inherited from EA-112.

Infrastructure Architecture principles are inherited from EA-320.

Integration Architecture principles are inherited from EA-340 through EA-345.

Enterprise Information Architecture principles are inherited from EA-350 through EA-359.

Enterprise Security Architecture principles are inherited from EA-360.

Enterprise Identity & Access Management Architecture principles are inherited from EA-361.

Enterprise Zero Trust Architecture principles are inherited from EA-362.

Enterprise Cryptography & PKI Architecture principles are inherited from EA-363.

Enterprise Secrets Management Architecture principles are inherited from EA-364.

All Enterprise Security Monitoring implementations shall comply with this standard.

---

# 1. Purpose

The purpose of this standard is to establish the Enterprise Security Monitoring & SIEM Architecture governing continuous monitoring, event collection, threat detection, security analytics and incident triggering across the MFM Enterprise Platform.

Enterprise Security Monitoring shall

- provide continuous visibility
- detect security threats
- support Zero Trust Architecture
- enable rapid incident response
- improve operational resilience
- support regulatory compliance
- centralize security telemetry
- strengthen Enterprise Security
- enable risk-based monitoring
- remain technology independent

Security Monitoring shall be implemented as a shared Enterprise capability.

---

# 2. Scope

This standard applies to all Enterprise security monitoring including

- Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)
- log management
- security event collection
- security analytics
- threat detection
- behavioral analytics
- cloud security monitoring
- Kubernetes security monitoring
- API security monitoring
- endpoint security monitoring
- network security monitoring
- identity monitoring
- workload monitoring
- compliance monitoring
- security dashboards

This standard applies regardless of deployment model, technology platform or hosting provider.

---

# 3. Enterprise Security Monitoring Principles

Enterprise Security Monitoring shall be governed by the following principles.

## Continuous Monitoring

Security events shall be collected, analyzed and monitored continuously.

---

## Centralized Visibility

Security telemetry shall be collected into centrally governed Enterprise monitoring services.

---

## Risk-Based Detection

Detection capabilities shall prioritize events based upon business risk, threat intelligence and operational impact.

---

## Zero Trust Monitoring

Every identity, workload, device and communication channel shall be continuously monitored.

No identity or workload shall be permanently trusted.

---

## Automation First

Threat detection, correlation, alerting and initial response shall be automated wherever practical.

---

## Technology Independence

Enterprise Security Monitoring shall remain independent of specific SIEM products, cloud providers and security vendors.

---

# 4. Enterprise Security Monitoring Objectives

The Enterprise Security Monitoring Architecture shall

- improve Enterprise visibility
- detect attacks rapidly
- reduce attacker dwell time
- support Security Operations
- improve compliance
- enable forensic investigations
- strengthen cyber resilience
- improve operational awareness
- support Enterprise governance
- enable continuous improvement

Security monitoring shall operate as an Enterprise-wide capability.

