# 6. Enterprise Security Monitoring Architecture

The Enterprise Security Monitoring Architecture defines the standardized framework for collecting, processing, analyzing and responding to security telemetry across the MFM Enterprise Platform.

The architecture consists of

- Enterprise SIEM Services
- Security Event Collection Services
- Log Management Services
- Event Correlation Services
- Threat Detection Services
- Security Analytics Services
- Threat Intelligence Services
- Security Dashboard Services
- Alert Management Services
- Security Data Lake

Security monitoring capabilities shall be implemented as reusable Enterprise services.

Business applications shall integrate with Enterprise monitoring capabilities rather than implementing isolated monitoring solutions.

---

# 7. Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)

The Enterprise SIEM shall serve as the central platform for security event collection, normalization, analysis and response.

The SIEM shall support

- centralized log collection
- event normalization
- event enrichment
- real-time analysis
- threat detection
- event correlation
- alert generation
- forensic investigations
- compliance reporting
- operational dashboards

The SIEM shall provide a unified security view across

- users
- applications
- APIs
- infrastructure
- cloud platforms
- Kubernetes clusters
- databases
- networks
- endpoints
- workloads

---

# 8. Security Event Collection

Security-relevant events shall be collected from all Enterprise systems.

Event sources include

- operating systems
- applications
- APIs
- IAM platforms
- authentication services
- firewalls
- cloud services
- Kubernetes platforms
- service mesh
- databases
- network devices
- endpoint security platforms
- CI/CD pipelines
- AI services

Security events shall be transmitted using secure and reliable mechanisms.

Loss of security telemetry shall be detected and reported.

---

# 9. Log Management

Enterprise log management shall provide consistent handling of all security logs.

Log management shall include

- log collection
- secure transport
- centralized storage
- indexing
- retention
- archival
- integrity validation
- encryption
- access control
- audit logging

Security logs shall be protected against unauthorized modification and deletion.

Retention periods shall comply with Enterprise governance and regulatory requirements.

---

# 10. Log Normalization

Security telemetry shall be normalized into a common Enterprise event model.

Normalization shall provide

- consistent timestamps
- standardized identities
- normalized event categories
- common severity levels
- standardized asset identifiers
- standardized location identifiers
- unified metadata
- correlation attributes
- threat indicators
- governance metadata

Normalization improves interoperability, analytics and cross-platform correlation.

---

# 11. Correlation Rules

Enterprise Security Monitoring shall correlate related security events to identify threats.

Correlation rules shall support

- authentication anomalies
- privilege escalation
- credential misuse
- suspicious API activity
- abnormal workload behavior
- lateral movement
- malware indicators
- ransomware indicators
- data exfiltration
- policy violations

Correlation rules shall be continuously reviewed and improved.

False positives and false negatives shall be monitored and minimized.

---

# 12. Threat Intelligence Integration

Enterprise Security Monitoring shall integrate with trusted threat intelligence sources.

Threat intelligence may include

- indicators of compromise (IOC)
- indicators of attack (IOA)
- malicious domains
- malicious IP addresses
- malware signatures
- ransomware intelligence
- vulnerability intelligence
- phishing intelligence
- adversary tactics
- MITRE ATT&CK mappings

Threat intelligence shall enrich security events and improve detection accuracy.

Threat intelligence sources shall be regularly validated and updated.

---

# 13. Security Monitoring Dependencies

Enterprise Security Monitoring depends upon

- Enterprise Security Architecture
- Enterprise IAM
- Enterprise Zero Trust
- Enterprise Cryptography & PKI
- Enterprise Secrets Management
